WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Russia’s Jewish community has been watching&nbsp;
the war in Ukraine with mounting alarm.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.500
But most have kept silent fearing state&nbsp;
reprisals if they spoke out against the Kremlin.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.420
And yet as the fighting intensified&nbsp;
a senior figure in the community felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.280
that silence was immoral. So he fled&nbsp;
Moscow after 30 years as the city’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.660
Chief Rabbi in order to speak freely&nbsp;
- and he joins me now from Berlin.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.640
He’s Pinchas Goldschmidt, President of&nbsp;
the Conference of European Rabbis and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.140
he wants all Jews in Russia&nbsp;
to get out now. His reasons?

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.080
It is a) the rise in antisemitism,&nbsp;
b) the possibility of the closing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.500
of the iron curtain, that it is&nbsp;
going to be impossible to leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:51.360
Number three are the harder-hitting sanctions&nbsp;
which are going to damage the economy long term.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.260
In Conflict Zone this week we ask&nbsp;
if Ukraine’s president Volodymyr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.280
Zelenskyy was right to criticize&nbsp;
Israel’s response to the war.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.600
Should Jews speak out whenever&nbsp;
universal values are threatened?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.020
And will historians look back on this war as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.040
wrong turning for Russia – or&nbsp;
the start of a new, dark age?

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:11.526
----------

00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:18.000
Rabbi Pinchas Goldschmidt,&nbsp;
welcome to Conflict Zone.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.700
Tim, thank you for having me on your program.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:27.300
It's good to see you. Your initial response to the&nbsp;
February invasion, if I can start with that, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.680
February invasion of Ukraine, was basically to say&nbsp;
nothing. Not to support it, not to condemn it. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.700
order, you said, not to get the Jewish community&nbsp;
into trouble. Do you regret that initial response?

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.140
Considering the shock of every&nbsp;
single member of the community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:56.520
and I think many of the citizens in Russia,&nbsp;
of the actual invasion, most people actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:02.940
thought that nothing is going to happen. I sat&nbsp;
the day before with many diplomats and members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.400
of parliament and others, and they said: Oh,&nbsp;
who needs Ukraine? We don't need Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:16.800
And so it was a shock and it took time till it&nbsp;
registered that we are in here for an all-out war.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.720
You said later you realized keeping quiet in such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.300
circumstances is morally wrong. And yet&nbsp;
that was the clear choice you made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.220
the immediate aftermath of the invasion.&nbsp;
I'm wondering why, was it out of habit?

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:46.380
It was-, we got, I would say, in the last years of&nbsp;
the current regime, we in the community basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.420
we're like the whole of Russian society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:59.580
we're depoliticized. Which means we did not&nbsp;
comment on political issues and we did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:10.260
get involved in politics in order not to get&nbsp;
into trouble. Because if you support one way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:18.120
or you do the other way, if you don't support the&nbsp;
regime, then you have problems. However, as the-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:30.360
we understood that we're dealing here with a&nbsp;
European war. With millions of refugees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:41.400
Jewish communities which are being destroyed after&nbsp;
being so difficult to rebuild after the communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:48.720
regime, after the breakup of the Soviet Union. I&nbsp;
came to the realization, and not everyone, I'm-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.900
I want to say this openly, that not only can we&nbsp;
stay silent, we have to do something about it.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:02.880
So in that case, have recent events in Russia&nbsp;
and your response to them compelled you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:09.900
rethink your attitude to morality? What is a&nbsp;
moral response to an invasion of this kind?

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:22.020
No, I don't think that-, I think that since the&nbsp;
changes which have happened in Russia had been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:29.700
I would say, gradual, it has not been from&nbsp;
one day to the next. So it was difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:38.280
to pinpoint to the moment that you had to say,&nbsp;
enough is enough. I cannot stay silent anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:50.460
And however, the change, the major change in&nbsp;
the country of waking up to 24th of February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.460 --> 00:05:01.320
in the morning and seeing with mass arrests and&nbsp;
every last independent media being shut down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:09.960
we understood that this was a major change and we&nbsp;
live in a different country with different rules.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.660
You say you were living in a different country,&nbsp;
but you lived in a country where President Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:21.300
had invaded and occupied Crimea in 2014.&nbsp;
He’d fueled a war in the Donbass where some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:28.800
15,000 people had died. So February 24th was&nbsp;
a continuation of what had gone before, wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.240
it? It was fully in line with the trajectory&nbsp;
that this administration had been following:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.220
expanding Russia's borders, clamping down on&nbsp;
civil liberties at home, and killing enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:43.800
political opponents for the rest to get the&nbsp;
idea. That much had to be clear, wasn't it?

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.600
Yes. First of all, when-, with the Crimea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:54.525
not one person died during the&nbsp;
takeover of the Crimea. And, you know-

00:05:54.525 --> 00:05:54.655
Does that make it ok?

00:05:54.655 --> 00:06:02.640
…I could throw the question back to you. Where was&nbsp;
Europe? Where was the United States? And with-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:11.160
why didn't they push all these very punishing&nbsp;
sanctions in 2014? Why did they wait till 2022?

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:17.940
Good question. And we put the question to them.&nbsp;
But I'm asking you, were you not aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.680
rights and universal rights were being trampled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.120
and people were being repressed and&nbsp;
killed in Russia during that period.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:38.460
It was-, I would say that, number one, the&nbsp;
people who were repressed was on a smaller scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:49.320
And Tim, I want to tell you that&nbsp;
the red line between a democracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:56.400
and authoritarianism is not always clear. And&nbsp;
when you pass from one system to the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:07.500
it's not always clear when is the red line passed?&nbsp;
So is it when there are no more free elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:20.520
or is it when some people critical of the regime&nbsp;
are being jailed? It's not always clear that-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.700
you know, and you have quite a few countries where&nbsp;
you pass from a democracy to an authoritarianism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:33.480
and then to totalitarianism. Are the lines clear?&nbsp;
The lines are not always clear. Only in hindsight.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:39.660
Is there a lesson that you took away&nbsp;
from this, that staying silent is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.880
an option where universal rights are being&nbsp;
trampled and people are being repressed?

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:55.860
I think that in today's situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:06.120
where a country has been invaded and there are&nbsp;
millions and millions of refugees leaving this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:15.360
country it is very important for everyone to&nbsp;
speak up. Everyone who can. Not everyone can.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.880
Looking back, do you think if more people had&nbsp;
spoken up, and you talked about the Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.140
reaction - you threw the question back&nbsp;
to me as if I was talking for the West,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:29.100
I'm not actually talking for the West.&nbsp;
But if more people had spoken out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:35.220
both in Russia and around the world, do&nbsp;
you think that President Putin might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.400
have gone ahead with the full-scale&nbsp;
invasion he launched in February?

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:55.020
I tend to think that if somebody would&nbsp;
have told on the 23rd of February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:12.420
to the Kremlin that the reaction of the West,&nbsp;
of Europe, the United States is going to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:20.760
one we have seen, I highly doubt that the&nbsp;
Kremlin would have gone on with this war.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:27.180
So is the Jewish community in Russia&nbsp;
now rethinking the practice of staying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.740
silent and keeping their head down and&nbsp;
trying to stay out of trouble? Should it?

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:40.800
You know as well as I am, as I do, that people&nbsp;
who speak up are being fined or being arrested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:48.360
The businesses are being closed down. So&nbsp;
it is very easy to criticize the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:54.480
regime from the safety of London or&nbsp;
Paris or Berlin. It is much harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.900
to do it if you live inside Russia, your&nbsp;
business is there, you have parents there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:03.840
you have children there. So it's much easier&nbsp;
to talk about it than actually to do it.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.540
But Rabbi Goldschmidt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:11.520
making the right moral decision can't&nbsp;
always be a cost-free exercise, can it?

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:19.980
Take-, Yes, it is-, Every person has&nbsp;
a free choice and every person has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:29.220
to take the right decision. So&nbsp;
I'm concerned about the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:34.920
of the Jewish community in Russia, and&nbsp;
I'm not the only one concerned. Thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.660
and tens of thousands of Jews have left the&nbsp;
country since the beginning of the invasion.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.056
And should those who stayed-

00:10:42.056 --> 00:10:44.880
And I think that by leaving they have-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:51.000
I'm sure that by leaving they-, everyone&nbsp;
who left the country made a statement.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:11:00.120
And should those who've left the country,&nbsp;
what should their attitude be now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.140
Should they speak out? Should they&nbsp;
say whatever they need to say?

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:13.920
I think that everyone who&nbsp;
has the possibility has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:24.060
state and has to say what he thinks, what&nbsp;
she thinks. And speaking to hundreds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:34.440
thousands of members of my community and also&nbsp;
of Russians of other religions, I feel that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:39.600
speaking with the people outside of Russia, I&nbsp;
feel there is very little support for this war.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:46.500
A prominent politician, former minister&nbsp;
Natan Sharansky, who was, as you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:50.100
a dissident who was jailed in&nbsp;
the Soviet Union - a refusenik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:55.560
as they were called at the time - he says that&nbsp;
if Jews have the chance to get out of Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.700
they should take it. They should&nbsp;
go. Do you support that view?

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:08.640
Yes, I support this view and for many&nbsp;
reasons. And the many reasons why Jews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:16.260
today are concerned about the future in Russia&nbsp;
it is a) the rise in antisemitism, b) the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:20.820
possibility of a closing of the iron curtain&nbsp;
that it is going to be impossible to leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:28.560
Number three are the harder-hitting sanctions&nbsp;
which are going to damage the economy long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.420
term. And number four the possibility of&nbsp;
that there's going to be a general draft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:42.480
for the Army and number five is the&nbsp;
widening repression of civil society.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.840
Let me, if I may, take you back to&nbsp;
the time when you made the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:56.400
in March to leave Russia. You said that after two&nbsp;
weeks soul searching, you left because you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.140
to be able to speak out against the war, and you'd&nbsp;
been told the Jewish community would suffer if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:07.620
did that and stayed. Can you clear up exactly what&nbsp;
threats were aimed at you personally and by whom?

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:19.140
I think that it was not only the possibility&nbsp;
of speaking out. Speaking out is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:28.620
but as you know, Judaism is a religion of deeds.&nbsp;
It's not only a religion of thoughts or beliefs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:37.380
It is the actual deed which is important for us.&nbsp;
And we call this deed the mitzvah. And speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:45.720
out has been secondary. First and most important&nbsp;
has been the feeling and the realization that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:52.080
we have to do something to help those thousands&nbsp;
of refugees of our communities who had to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:58.680
to Eastern Europe, which was with maybe with one&nbsp;
suitcase, who didn't have a roof on their heads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:05.880
And that's what we did. We established under the&nbsp;
aegis of the Conference of European Rabbis this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:11.580
fund, an international fund to help Ukrainian&nbsp;
refugees. And I've been visiting, I left Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:18.720
to visit in Eastern European capitals, in&nbsp;
Budapest, in Warsaw and also in Romania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:24.720
all those thousands of Jewish refugees and&nbsp;
other refugees who went there. And we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:30.060
established programs for the integration and for&nbsp;
the help of those refugees. So speaking out has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:38.280
been secondary. The number one mitzvah or the&nbsp;
number one deed, which we had to do is to help.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:44.460
My question was about the threats that&nbsp;
you personally received. How graphic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:46.500
how clear were these threats?

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:53.640
I think if you are a clergyman&nbsp;
living in a certain country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.640 --> 00:15:00.660
you can identify, you understand when&nbsp;
you're getting messages from certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:05.460
quarters. And it doesn't always have to&nbsp;
be personal, it can be communal as well.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.520
So what was said to you and by whom?

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:15.300
I don't want to go into more details. There&nbsp;
are still many members of the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:22.020
living in Russia continuing to work there&nbsp;
with people in the clergy. We have rabbis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:26.340
we have community leaders. At this&nbsp;
moment, I don't want to go into details.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:32.040
One or two Jewish leaders in Russia&nbsp;
did speak out and did refuse to tow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:36.960
the official line. Couldn't you have&nbsp;
done the same and stayed in Russia?

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:46.980
It's-, I don't think that I would have&nbsp;
been-, I don't think I would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:53.460
able to help and to establish this whole&nbsp;
structure to help refugees staying in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:58.380
Russia. And I don't think I would have&nbsp;
been able to communicate to the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:06.360
to the Jewish world, and to the world at large&nbsp;
the issues and the challenges we are facing.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:11.400
Did leaving Russia feel in&nbsp;
any way like running away?

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:22.680
I think that if I've been the only one&nbsp;
making this decision, maybe you're right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:34.380
I'm-, I can tell you one thing. I have a very good&nbsp;
friend. She's one of the leading journalists in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:41.880
the opposition. And initially she was quite upset&nbsp;
that I left. But a month later, she left herself.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.240
So you felt vindicated?

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:55.200
It's not a question of feeling vindicated.&nbsp;
It is a question of today we have tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.200 --> 00:17:00.900
thousands of members of my community who are&nbsp;
living outside Russia. I would say if we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:05.520
talking about people who used to come to our&nbsp;
synagogues, I would say even the majority is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:11.100
outside of Russia. My community is all over the&nbsp;
world. They are in Dubai, they are in Israel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:20.700
they are in London and in Germany. And&nbsp;
the immigration to Israel from Russia was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:29.760
twice as high than from Ukraine in the last-,&nbsp;
since the war started. And I'm responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:35.040
the wider community. And I also believe&nbsp;
that I'll be able to help our community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:39.240
which stayed on in Russia better from&nbsp;
the outside than from the inside.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:46.560
Rabbi Goldschmidt, it seems that relations between&nbsp;
senior Jewish figures in Russia and Ukraine have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:54.480
been badly damaged by the events in recent months.&nbsp;
In March, Ukraine's Chief Rabbi, Moshe Azman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:59.520
roundly condemned leaders of the Russian Jewish&nbsp;
community and said your failure to denounce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:06.300
invasion was tantamount to complicity. How&nbsp;
hard is it going to be to repair those ties?

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:12.240
I don't know. There were some leaders&nbsp;
who supported the invasion. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:18.300
want to express myself, I don't want&nbsp;
to judge them. Who am I to judge? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:21.780
can tell you that my relationship with&nbsp;
our Ukrainian colleagues is wonderful.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:30.720
And did you call Rabbi Azman? He says-, when&nbsp;
he said: ‘I'm talking to you, dear Russians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:36.540
dear Jews. Remember that one who is indifferent&nbsp;
is an accomplice to war crimes, crimes against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:42.900
humanity. I stand with the Holy Torah and I&nbsp;
tell you to wake up.' Hardly anyone, he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:49.440
has called me. Do you understand the sense of&nbsp;
abandonment that he had from the Jewish community?

00:18:52.680 --> 00:19:00.000
I for sure, I for sure can feel it.&nbsp;
Ukraine doesn't have only one Chief Rabbi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.880
by the way. You know, we have quite-, we have, I&nbsp;
think, three Chief Rabbis in Ukraine and we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:13.140
in touch with them. And I myself just three&nbsp;
weeks ago spent a whole long weekend in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:19.200
town of Neptun in Romania with 1200 refugees&nbsp;
from Odessa, with the Chief Rabbi of Odesa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:28.680
So we are in touch with our colleagues from&nbsp;
Ukraine. And I also spoke a few days ago to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:34.260
a group of new immigrants from Russia and Ukraine&nbsp;
in Jerusalem. And you had over there in the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:41.160
you had about 40 or 50 young people. And they&nbsp;
came from Moscow, from other cities in Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:46.860
and from Odesa and from Kyiv and from Mykolaiv,&nbsp;
even from Mariupol. And you should see those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:52.500
people just sitting together trying to help each&nbsp;
other in the new country. They all left their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:58.260
countries of origins, not because they wanted&nbsp;
to, but because of the impossibility to stay.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:06.900
Talking of helping Ukrainians, President&nbsp;
Zelenskyy, himself a Jew, has had harsh words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:13.740
for what he sees as Israel's inadequate response&nbsp;
to the conflict. To Israel's mediation efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:19.500
he said there can be no mediation between&nbsp;
good and evil. He has a point, doesn't he?

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:26.340
I want to tell you, those people who&nbsp;
want to criticize Israel for not going-,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.360
not joining Europe with sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:41.400
against Russia. I want to remind them that Israel&nbsp;
is not buying oil and gas from Russia. Israel is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:46.680
not financing the war. It is Europe buying gas and&nbsp;
oil from Russia, is financing the war in Russia.

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:53.580
Some of that criticism, Rabbi Goldschmidt,&nbsp;
has come from inside Israel itself. The former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:57.600
Prime Minister, Ehud Olmert, for instance.&nbsp;
He didn't think much of the government's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.740
reaction in February. There are times, he&nbsp;
said, when you have to decide who you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:07.560
what you stand for. You can't fool around,&nbsp;
you can't fool yourself. If Israel were like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:11.820
America trying to rally its allies, would&nbsp;
we like our best friends saying, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:16.620
there are pros and cons and we've got other&nbsp;
things to think about? He's not saying do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:21.480
the easy thing. He's saying do the right thing.&nbsp;
You have no sympathy with him, with his views?

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:32.040
The same way that Europe is trying to do the&nbsp;
right thing, but obviously it is impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:39.300
leave elderly people or children in unheated homes&nbsp;
in the middle of December and January, the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:47.040
way Israel cannot leave millions of people prone&nbsp;
to attacks from rockets, from Hezbollah and from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:56.040
the Iranian sponsored guerrillas in Lebanon and&nbsp;
Syria. So each country wants to join the sanction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.040 --> 00:22:03.480
regime or to criticize. However, each country has&nbsp;
their priorities to protect their citizens first.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:11.160
But Rabbi Goldschmidt, Ukrainians are fighting&nbsp;
for their lives and the survival of their unitary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:16.260
state. And Israel is saying: Yeah, well, it's&nbsp;
terrible, but we've got more important security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:22.620
concerns to worry about with Iran and Syria.&nbsp;
You said in July: 'Nowadays, the world is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:28.020
looking at Israel and the Jewish people and&nbsp;
expects us to be on the moral high ground.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:33.840
And I'm just wondering whether this position&nbsp;
is the moral high ground you want to occupy.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:42.720
Within Israel itself, you have a discussion&nbsp;
among-, it might become an issue in the upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:47.400
elections in November, among political leaders to&nbsp;
what extent Israel should be involved or should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:59.220
not be involved in this conflict. So Israel,&nbsp;
I would say, is-, right now has taken a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:04.800
position. Yair Lapid, the current prime minister&nbsp;
of Israel, has taken a position criticizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:13.200
Russia. And I want to tell you, Tim, that Israel&nbsp;
has had problems because of it. Russia decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:18.180
close the Jewish Agency. This didn't happen from&nbsp;
one day to the next. There was a reason for it.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:23.040
Do you regard the changes&nbsp;
in Russia as irreversible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:27.900
Rabbi Goldschmidt? Will historians&nbsp;
look back on this, in your view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:33.120
as a wrong turn for Russia or the beginning&nbsp;
of a new, dark age in the country?

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:44.100
Right now, I think that Russia is&nbsp;
entering a period of deep isolation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:55.680
a new almost totally closed Iron Curtain&nbsp;
with Europe. Not with Asia, but with Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.280 --> 00:24:04.140
And I think that the coming period&nbsp;
is going to be very difficult.

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.280
Do you foresee ever going&nbsp;
back? Do you want to go back?

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:15.180
As you know, we have to be optimists. And I&nbsp;
hope that one day I'll be able to get back.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:19.320
Rabbi Pinchas Goldschmidt, it's been good to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:22.560
you on Conflict Zone. Thank you very&nbsp;
much indeed for your time. Thank you.

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:24.960
Thank you. Thank you, Tim, for inviting me.

